1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Good morning.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,640
The subject of today's lecture is options.

3
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,080
And I think maybe I'd better

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,800
first define what an option is

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,240
before I move to just saying anything about them.

6
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,600
Because some of you may not have encountered them,

7
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:23,600
because they're not part of everyday life for most people.

8
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,040
Although they are in a sense.

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:26,000
I'll get back to that.

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,240
Let me just define terms here.

11
00:00:31,759 --> 00:00:37,760
So, I should turn the light on here.

12
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:41,240
I don't know where the switch is.

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,240
Well, I will just make do.

14
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,240
So, there's two kinds of options.

15
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:53,240
There's a call and a put.

16
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,240
And the call is a call.

17
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,000
And the put is a call.

18
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
OK.

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,180
A call option is an

20
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:04,360
option to buy something at a

21
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:07,800
specified price.

22
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,940
And the price is called

23
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:16,080
the exercise price or a strike

24
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:18,200
price.

25
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,600
Those are synonyms.

26
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,200
And a put option is the

27
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,800
right to sell something at the, I

28
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
don't know, specified exercise price.

29
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:38,000
And it has another term that has to be specified.

30
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
And that's the exercise date.

31
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:51,000
OK.

32
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Options go back thousands of years.

33
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
OK.

34
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,500
And the price is, it must

35
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,000
have happened before we have any

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
recorded records.

37
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
If you want to buy something, you're thinking of buying

38
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
something from someone, but you

39
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
don't want to put up the

40
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,500
money today, you go to some

41
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,000
lawyer and say, write up a

42
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
contract, I want to buy

43
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
an option to buy this thing.

44
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
So, for example, if you

45
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
are thinking of building a building

46
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,500
that is owned now by a farmer,

47
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:30,000
but you're not ready to do it,

48
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,500
you may be thinking about it,

49
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:35,000
you can go to the farmer and

50
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,500
say, I'd like to buy

51
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:40,000
that corner of that acre there.

52
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
I'd like to have the right to buy it.

53
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
I'll pay you now for the right.

54
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And you get a lawyer

55
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,000
and you write up a contract.

56
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And that's an option.

57
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
You have an option to buy.

58
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,500
And you can do that

59
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,000
at the exercise price until the

60
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
exercise date.

61
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Now, in modern terminology, we have two kinds, American and

62
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
European.

63
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And it doesn't refer to geography, those two terms.

64
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
The terms refer instead to the price.

65
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And the price refers to when you can exercise.

66
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
So, the American option is better than the European

67
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
option for the buyer, because the American option can be

68
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
exercised at any time until the exercise date.

69
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Whereas a European option can be exercised only on the

70
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
exercise date.

71
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
But you see, the American

72
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
option has to be better, or

73
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,000
not worse than.

74
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
It's strictly better, but not worse than a European option,

75
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,000
because you have more options.

76
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:05,000
So, I think we've defined what they are.

77
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Do you understand well enough what they are?

78
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
They occur naturally in life.

79
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
I remember, Avinash Dixit was writing about options.

80
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
And he said, well, when you're dating someone, and you're

81
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,500
not going to marry them, and

82
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:23,000
you know the person will marry

83
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,500
you, you have an option, which

84
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,000
you can exercise at any time by

85
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
agreeing to marry.

86
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Now, one of the theorems

87
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
in option theory is, you usually

88
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
don't want to exercise a call option early.

89
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,500
And so, Dixit was saying, well,

90
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:46,000
maybe that's why a lot of

91
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
people have trouble getting married.

92
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
They don't want to exercise their option early.

93
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
What we'll see is that options have option value.

94
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
They give you a choice.

95
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,000
And so, there's something there.

96
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
When you exercise an option, that means when you actually

97
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
buy the thing, or in the case

98
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
of a put, sell the thing, then

99
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
you're losing the choice.

100
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
So, you've given up something.

101
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Of course, you have to also exercise eventually, if things

102
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
are going to make sense.

103
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
But usually, when we talk about options, we're talking

104
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,500
now about options to buy a

105
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:24,000
share of stock, or 100 shares

106
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
of stock.

107
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
And that's the usual example.

108
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
But they occur all over the place.

109
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Let me mention some other examples of options.

110
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
The usual story is the stock option.

111
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,500
You go to your broker, and you

112
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:45,000
say, I'd like to buy an option

113
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
to buy 100 shares of Microsoft.

114
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:49,000
I want to buy Microsoft.

115
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
I want to buy an option to buy Microsoft.

116
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Which happens to be cheaper, by the way, usually.

117
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,500
Usually, it costs more money

118
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:00,000
to actually buy the thing than

119
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,500
to buy the right to

120
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,000
buy the thing at another price.

121
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
We'll get back to that.

122
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
But in a sense, let's think about this.

123
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Stocks themselves are options, in a sense, with a zero

124
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
exercise price.

125
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Maybe I'll have to get back to that.

126
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
I can explain that.

127
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,500
But what I mean is, let me

128
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:26,000
get back and explain that in a

129
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
minute.

130
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
But let me go ahead to other examples.

131
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Mortgages, an ordinary home mortgage has an option

132
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
characteristic to it, in the sense

133
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
that if the price of your

134
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,500
home drops a lot, you can

135
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:46,000
just walk away from the mortgage

136
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,000
and say, I'm going to buy this.

137
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
And say, I'm out of here.

138
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
It's like not exercising an option.

139
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
It's analogous.

140
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Or I can choose to prepay a mortgage early.

141
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And that's like exercising an option.

142
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
So, option pricing gets into all sorts of things.

143
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Okay.

144
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
So, I thought I should say

145
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
something about the purposes of options.

146
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
I'm not sure if I'm saying it correctly.

147
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,000
I'm not sure if I'm saying it correctly.

148
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
I'm not sure if I'm saying it correctly.

149
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
But I'm going to give you

150
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
a couple of examples of options

151
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
before I move on to

152
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
try to discuss what their properties

153
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
and pricing are, which is

154
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
the main subject of this lecture.

155
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Two different justifications for options.

156
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Why do we have options?

157
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Some people cynically think that

158
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
options are just gambling vehicles.

159
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
It's another way to gamble.

160
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
You can go to the casino, you

161
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
can play poker, or you can buy options.

162
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,500
Well, I think for some

163
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,000
people, that's just what it is.

164
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,500
They're volatile, risky investments that can

165
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:03,000
make you a lot of money.

166
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
But I think they have a basic purpose.

167
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
And so, or purposes.

168
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
First of all, theoretical.

169
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
If we're trying to design the

170
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
ideal financial system, what would we do?

171
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:28,000
And I think back, some people thought

172
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,000
of ideal economic systems without reference to finance.

173
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,500
Like Karl Marx, I come back to him, the great

174
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:42,000
communist, thought that we would have an ideal communist state.

175
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
And there'd be no financial markets.

176
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
When they actually tried it,

177
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
and they tried to do it,

178
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
I think they gradually realized

179
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
that not having any financial markets

180
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,500
makes our entrepreneurship, our management

181
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:03,000
of enterprises kind of blind.

182
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
We can't see where we're going, because there's no prices.

183
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
We don't know what anything is worth.

184
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
There was an old joke that the

185
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
communist countries survived only because they had money.

186
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:13,000
And that's not true.

187
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
The communist countries survived only because they

188
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
had prices from capitalist countries to rely on.

189
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Otherwise, they don't know anything about values or profits.

190
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
So, we need prices.

191
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,500
And I just traced the,

192
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:33,000
many people have written about this.

193
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
But I mentioned in 1964, Kenneth

194
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Arrow, who is an economic theorist,

195
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:47,000
wrote a classic paper in which he argued that,

196
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:52,000
unless we have prices for all states of nature,

197
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
there is a sense in

198
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
which the economic system is inefficient.

199
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,500
You really need the price of

200
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:03,000
everything, including the price of some possibility.

201
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
And in a sense, that's what options are giving you.

202
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
The existence of options.

203
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
The existence of options are giving you.

204
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
So, Steven Ross, who used to teach

205
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
here at Yale, a friend of mine,

206
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
lives here in New Haven.

207
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:33,000
In 1976, in the Quarterly Journal of Economics,

208
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
wrote a classic paper about options,

209
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
showing that, in a sense, they complete the state space.

210
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
They create prices for

211
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
everything that affects decision making.

212
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,500
So, I'm not going to get

213
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,000
into the technicalities of the paper,

214
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
but I wanted to start

215
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
with a theoretical justification for options,

216
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
so that you'll see why we're doing it.

217
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,500
I don't want this to come across as a

218
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,000
lecture, how you can gamble in the options market.

219
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
This is about making things work right for the economy.

220
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Making things work right for the economic system.

221
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Improving human welfare.

222
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
But it seems, a lot of people don't get that.

223
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
That's why Karl Marx was so successful.

224
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
It seems too abstract.

225
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
What does this options market do for us?

226
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
But let me just go back

227
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
to the example I started out with.

228
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,500
You're a construction firm, and

229
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:30,000
you're thinking of building something.

230
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,000
A new supermarket, right?

231
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Where people can buy their food.

232
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And you note that there's a

233
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
pair of expressways crossing somewhere.

234
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
And you think, that's the perfect

235
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
place to build a mega supermarket.

236
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Because everyone can get there by car.

237
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
And there's a lot of land.

238
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
I can build a big parking lot.

239
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Perfect.

240
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
But before you think further, you go

241
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
to buy an option on the land, right?

242
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,500
So, you knock on the door at the

243
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:59,000
farmhouse, and there's a farmer with all these acres.

244
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
And you say, I'm thinking

245
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
of building a mega supermarket here.

246
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
I'd like to buy an option on your farm.

247
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
You learn something at that moment.

248
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
You might learn that the farmer

249
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
says, I've already sold an option.

250
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
So, I can't do it.

251
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,500
You can try to talk to the person I sold

252
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:19,000
it to, and see if you can buy it from him.

253
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,500
Or the farmer might say, I've had three other offers, and

254
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:28,000
I'm raising my price to some millions of millions of dollars.

255
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Then you have a second thoughts about doing it.

256
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,000
You see what I'm saying?

257
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
That the price discovery is in there.

258
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
It's making things happen differently.

259
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
You're learning something.

260
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
The farmer is learning something.

261
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
You are learning something from the options market.

262
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And ultimately, it decides where that supermarket will go.

263
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So, that's the theoretical purpose of options.

264
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,000
But I wanted to talk also

265
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
about a behavioral purpose of options.

266
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:12,000
It gets a little fuzzier about the

267
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:19,000
actual benefits of options from this standpoint.

268
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
The behavioral theory of options says that, well, it's very,

269
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,000
very many different aspects of human behavior tie into options.

270
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
But,

271
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
I would say it has something

272
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,000
to do with attention anomalies and salience.

273
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Psychologists talk about this, that people make mistakes

274
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
very commonly in what they pay attention to,

275
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
what strikes their fancy or their imagination.

276
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Salience is something psychologists also talk about.

277
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
That certain kinds of events,

278
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
maybe it's similar to salience.

279
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
But salience events are events that tend

280
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
to attract attention, tend to be remembered.

281
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,500
Now, when you think of options, a lot

282
00:14:17,500 --> 00:14:23,000
of options are what are called incentive options.

283
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,500
And when you get your

284
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:26,000
first job, you may discover this.

285
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
They'll give you options to buy

286
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
shares in the company you work for.

287
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Why do they do that?

288
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
I think it's because of certain human behavioral traits

289
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
that I mentioned here, your attention and your salience.

290
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
But it's not necessarily very expensive for a company

291
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
to give you options to buy shares in the company.

292
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
But it gives you, puts you in a situation where

293
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
you start to pay attention to the value of the company.

294
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
It becomes salient for you.

295
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
And you start hoping that the price of the company will go

296
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
up, because you have options to buy it at a strike price.

297
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
You hope that the company's price per share goes above

298
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
your strike price, because then your options are worth something.

299
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
They're in the money.

300
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
So, it may change your motivation and your morale

301
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
at work, or sense of identity with the company.

302
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
All these sorts of things figure in.

303
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And so, that's why we have incentive options.

304
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
They can also give you peace of mind.

305
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:39,500
And insurance is actually related to options in the sense that when you

306
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:47,000
buy insurance on your house, it's like buying a put option on your house.

307
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,500
Although it may be not directly

308
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:52,000
connected to the home's value, right?

309
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,500
When you buy an insurance policy on your house and

310
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:59,000
the house burns down, you collect on the insurance policy.

311
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Well, the price of your house fell to zero.

312
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
If you had bought a put option on

313
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
the house, it would do the same thing, right?

314
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
You would have an option to sell it

315
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
at a high price, something that's now worthless.

316
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
So, insurance is like options.

317
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And insurance gives you peace of mind.

318
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
And so, people think in certain repetitive patterns.

319
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
And one of them is that I

320
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
would like to not worry about something.

321
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
So, I can get peace of mind by not.

322
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
But I have a put option on

323
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
something that I might otherwise worry about.

324
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Maybe that's enough of an introduction.

325
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,500
But I've given you both theoretical

326
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,000
reasons for options and behavioral reasons.

327
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:50,000
I think of them as basically inevitable.

328
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,500
You may have people advising you

329
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,000
not to bother with options markets.

330
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
And that might be right for you in a sense.

331
00:16:58,000 --> 00:16:59,500
But I think that they're

332
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:01,000
always going to be with us.

333
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:05,000
And so, it's something that we have to understand.

