a put, it costs you $3.60.

414
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
And we want to try to understand these prices.

415
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:31,000
Okay.

416
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,000
That's the purpose here.

417
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:40,000
So, let me say one thing

418
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:46,000
more before I get into that.

419
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
This is presented for the potential buyer.

420
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Okay.

421
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:51,000
These are options prices.

422
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
And the seller of the option.

423
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
They are called the writer of the option.

424
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
I gave you an example before when I talked

425
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
about the farmer and you thinking of building a supermarket.

426
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,500
So, you are the buyer of the option

427
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:11,000
and the farmer is the writer of the option.

428
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:16,000
The farmer is writing the option to you.

429
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,500
You could also consider buying an option from

430
00:22:18,500 --> 00:22:21,000
someone else who is not even the farmer, right?

431
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
It could be some speculator.

432
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:24,000
You don't have to go to the farmer.

433
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:27,000
You could go to somebody else and say, I'd

434
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:30,000
like to buy an option on that farm over there.

435
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,000
And someone says, sure, I'll

436
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,000
sell you an option on it.

437
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,000
And then I'm good for it.

438
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
That means I have to go and

439
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
buy it at whatever price from the farmer.

440
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:38,000
Maybe that's not such a good idea.

441
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:41,000
He might sense my urgency to buy it.

442
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,500
But if it's a stock, someone can write

443
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:46,000
an option who doesn't even own the stock.

444
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,000
And so, that's called a naked seller of an option.

445
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Okay.

446
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,500
Neither the buyer nor the seller

447
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:55,000
ever have to trade in the stock.

448
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
This is a market by itself.

449
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,500
You know, you could buy an option and then you

450
00:23:00,500 --> 00:23:04,000
could sell it before as an option without ever exercising it.

451
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:09,500
And so, the writer could write an option and then buy an option

452
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:15,000
to cancel it out later and then essentially get out of that contract.

453
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:18,000
So, the option becomes a market of its own.

454
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Where prices are different.

455
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
And the prices of options start

456
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
to look like an independent market.

457
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:28,000
And this is called a derivatives market.

458
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:34,500
Because there's an underlying stock price, but

459
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:41,000
this is a derivative of the stock price.

460
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:45,000
They were first traded, the first options

461
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,000
exchange was the Chicago Board Options Exchange.

462
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:54,000
Which came in in 1973.

463
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
Before that, options were traded.

464
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
But they were traded through brokers.

465
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
And they didn't have the same presence.

466
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:03,500
You know, you didn't see

467
00:24:03,500 --> 00:24:05,000
all these options prices in newspapers.

468
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:08,500
It's when they opened the market for options

469
00:24:08,500 --> 00:24:12,000
that the options trading became a big thing.

470
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,000
So, options markets are relatively new.

471
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,000
I mean, if you consider 73 new.

472
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:19,000
You weren't born then.

473
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:23,000
But it's not really that long ago.

474
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Since then, there are many more options exchanges.

475
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:30,000
But CBOE is the first one.

476
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,000
They're now all over the world.

477
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:38,000
We also have options on futures.

478
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
And so, futures exchanges now routinely

479
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
trade options on their futures contracts.

480
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
So, that's a derivative on a derivative.

481
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,000
But it's done.

482
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:55,000
Okay.

483
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:59,000
So, let me draw a simple picture of option pricing.

484
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:06,000
So, this is the stock price.

485
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:12,000
And this is the option price.

486
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,000
Okay.

487
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:29,000
And I'm going to mark here the exercise price.

488
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:35,000
Then, let's look at the exercise date.

489
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
The last day.

490
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Okay, the option is about to expire.

491
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
And this is your last chance to buy the stock.

492
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:44,000
Then it doesn't matter on that

493
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:45,000
day whether it's an American or European.

494
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:46,000
It doesn't matter.

495
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:47,000
It doesn't matter whether it's

496
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:48,000
an American or European option.

497
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
They're both the same on the last day.

498
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,500
What is the price of the option

499
00:25:53,500 --> 00:25:56,000
as a function of the stock price?

500
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,000
Well, if the stock price is less

501
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:02,000
than the exercise price, the option is worthless.

502
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:03,000
Right?

503
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
It will not be exercised.

504
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:08,000
You won't exercise an option to buy it for

505
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:11,000
more than you could just buy it on the market.

506
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:12,000
Right?

507
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:13,000
You have to say call.

508
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Did I not say call?

509
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:15,000
Yes.

510
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:16,000
I'm going to put it up here.

511
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:19,000
We're talking about call options.

512
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:22,000
Thank you.

513
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:28,000
But if it's above the exercise price, this is a

514
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:34,000
45-degree angle that's in line with the slope of 1.

515
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:38,000
The option price rises with the stock price.

516
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
In fact, it just equals the

517
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
stock price minus the exercise price.

518
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,000
Right?

519
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:47,000
So, this region, we say, is out of the money.

520
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:51,500
The option is out of the money when its price

521
00:26:51,500 --> 00:26:56,000
is for a call, is less than the exercise price.

522
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
Here, it's in the money.

523
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:02,000
I'll put it up here, in the money.

524
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:05,000
And then, on the exercise date, it will

525
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:08,000
always equal the stock price minus the exercise price.

526
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
So, it's very simple.

527
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
Now, one confusion that's often made.

528
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,500
I gave the example of building a

529
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:17,000
shopping center or a supermarket on a farm.

530
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Now, someone might think that you buy an option on it, so

531
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:23,000
that you can think about it and make up your mind later.

532
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:26,000
Well, in a sense, you could do that.

533
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:30,500
But the thing is, you will exercise the option, whether or not

534
00:27:30,500 --> 00:27:35,000
you build the shopping mall or the supermarket, if it's in the money.

535
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Right?

536
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
Suppose the farmer, suppose you've changed your mind

537
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
and I don't want to build a supermarket.

538
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:41,000
But I'm sitting on a farm.

539
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:44,500
But I'm sitting on an option that I bought to buy his

540
00:27:44,500 --> 00:27:48,000
land for a price, which is less than the market price for it.

541
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
