871
00:44:47,000 --> 00:44:50,000
And if I do that, I've got a riskless investment.

872
00:44:50,000 --> 00:44:52,000
All right?

873
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:56,000
So, set these equal to each other.

874
00:44:56,000 --> 00:44:58,500
And that implies something about H.

875
00:44:58,500 --> 00:45:01,000
We can derive what H is.

876
00:45:01,000 --> 00:45:03,500
I just put these two equal

877
00:45:03,500 --> 00:45:06,000
to each other and solve for H.

878
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:08,000
And I get H equals UHS.

879
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:15,500
So, I've got C sub U minus C

880
00:45:15,500 --> 00:45:22,000
sub D all over U minus D S. Okay?

881
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:26,000
So, I've been able to put together a portfolio

882
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:30,000
of the stock and the option that has zero risk.

883
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:34,000
It's going to, if I do this, if I hold this

884
00:45:34,000 --> 00:45:38,000
amount of shares in my portfolio, I've got a riskless portfolio.

885
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:41,500
So, that means that the riskless

886
00:45:41,500 --> 00:45:45,000
portfolio has to earn the riskless rate.

887
00:45:45,000 --> 00:45:46,000
Right?

888
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:49,000
It's the same thing as a riskless rate.

889
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:51,000
So, it has to earn that.

890
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:53,000
If I can erase this now.

891
00:45:53,000 --> 00:45:56,000
I'm almost there to option pricing.

892
00:45:56,000 --> 00:46:00,500
The option pricing then says that

893
00:46:00,500 --> 00:46:05,000
since I've derived what H is,

894
00:46:05,000 --> 00:46:07,000
the portfolio has to be worth 1.5.

895
00:46:07,000 --> 00:46:13,500
So, I'm going to put in the value of 1 plus the

896
00:46:13,500 --> 00:46:20,000
interest rate times what I put in, which is HS minus C.

897
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:27,500
And that has to equal the value of the portfolio at the end,

898
00:46:27,500 --> 00:46:35,000
which is either UHS minus C sub U or DHS minus C sub D.

899
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:36,000
The same thing.

900
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:39,000
Okay?

901
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:44,000
So, I've already derived what H is.

902
00:46:44,000 --> 00:46:46,000
And I substitute it into that.

903
00:46:46,000 --> 00:46:49,000
And I solve for C.

904
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:56,000
So, substitute H in and solve for C.

905
00:46:56,000 --> 00:46:58,000
And we get the call price.

906
00:46:58,000 --> 00:47:00,000
Okay?

907
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:02,000
And it's kind of a little bit complicated.

908
00:47:02,000 --> 00:47:04,000
But I'll write it.

909
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:06,000
Call option price has to equal 1.5.

910
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:19,500
And it has to equal 1 plus R minus D all over U minus D times C sub U over 1

911
00:47:19,500 --> 00:47:33,000
plus R plus U minus 1 minus R all over U minus D times C sub D all over 1 plus R.

912
00:47:33,000 --> 00:47:35,000
And I'll put a box around that.

913
00:47:35,000 --> 00:47:39,000
Because that's our option price formula.

914
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:41,000
Okay?

915
00:47:41,000 --> 00:47:45,000
Did you follow all that?

916
00:47:45,000 --> 00:47:51,500
This is derived, this option price formula

917
00:47:51,500 --> 00:47:58,000
was derived from a no arbitrage condition.

918
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:03,000
Arbitrage in finance means riskless profit opportunity.

919
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:07,500
And the no arbitrage condition says it's never

920
00:48:07,500 --> 00:48:12,000
possible to make more than the riskless rate risklessly.

921
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:13,000
Alright?

922
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:15,000
If I could, suppose I had some way.

923
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:18,000
Suppose the riskless rate is 5%.

924
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:21,000
And I can make 6% risklessly.

925
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:24,000
Then I will borrow at the riskless

926
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:27,000
rate and put it into the 6% opportunity.

927
00:48:27,000 --> 00:48:29,000
And I'll do that till kingdom come.

928
00:48:29,000 --> 00:48:31,000
There's no limit to how much I'll do that.

929
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:32,000
I'll do it forever.

930
00:48:32,000 --> 00:48:35,500
And so, it can't, it's too much

931
00:48:35,500 --> 00:48:39,000
of a profit opportunity to ever happen.

932
00:48:39,000 --> 00:48:44,000
So, one of the most powerful insights of theoretical

933
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:49,000
finance is that the no arbitrage condition should hold.

934
00:48:49,000 --> 00:48:50,500
It's like saying there are

935
00:48:50,500 --> 00:48:52,000
no $10 bills on the pavement.

936
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:56,500
When you walk down the street and you see a $10 bill lying there

937
00:48:56,500 --> 00:49:01,000
on the street, your first thought ought to be, are my eyes deceiving me?

938
00:49:01,000 --> 00:49:03,000
Because somebody else would have picked

939
00:49:03,000 --> 00:49:05,000
it up if it were there.

940
00:49:05,000 --> 00:49:07,000
How can it be there?

941
00:49:07,000 --> 00:49:09,000
I once actually had that experience.

942
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:11,000
I was walking down the street in New York

943
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:13,000
and I saw it was actually a $5 bill.

944
00:49:13,000 --> 00:49:15,000
It was just lying there on the street.

945
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:17,000
And so, I reached down to pick

946
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:19,000
it up and then suddenly it disappeared.

947
00:49:19,000 --> 00:49:22,000
And it was people on one of the stoops

948
00:49:22,000 --> 00:49:25,000
of one of these New York townhouses playing a game.

949
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:28,000
They had tied a string to a $5 bill.

950
00:49:28,000 --> 00:49:30,000
And they would leave it on the street.

951
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:31,500
And watch people reach for

952
00:49:31,500 --> 00:49:33,000
it and they'd snatch it away.

953
00:49:33,000 --> 00:49:35,500
That's the only time in my life I

954
00:49:35,500 --> 00:49:38,000
ever saw a $5 bill on the pavement.

955
00:49:38,000 --> 00:49:40,500
And so, you know, it sounds like a pretty

956
00:49:40,500 --> 00:49:43,000
good assumption that if you see one, it isn't real.

957
00:49:43,000 --> 00:49:45,000
And that's all this is saying.

958
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:47,500
If the option price didn't follow

959
00:49:47,500 --> 00:49:50,000
this formula, something would be wrong.

960
00:49:50,000 --> 00:49:55,000
And so, it had better follow this formula.

961
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:58,000
Now, that is the basic core option theory.

962
00:49:58,000 --> 00:50:00,000
Now, the interesting thing about this theory is,

963
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:03,500
I didn't use the probability of

964
00:50:03,500 --> 00:50:07,000
up and the probability of down.

965
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:09,000
So, somebody said, wait a minute.

966
00:50:09,000 --> 00:50:12,000
My whole intuition about options is, I buy

967
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:15,000
an option because it might be in the money.

968
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:20,000
When I was just describing here, this is 45 cents.

969
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:23,000
I said, that's not much

970
00:50:23,000 --> 00:50:26,000
because it probably won't exceed $25.

971
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:28,000
It's so far below it.

972
00:50:28,000 --> 00:50:29,000
So, that's the problem.

973
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:34,000
So, it seems like the options should really

974
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:39,000
be fundamentally tied to the probability of success.

975
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:41,000
But it's not here at all.

976
00:50:41,000 --> 00:50:43,000
There's no probability in it.

977
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:44,000
You saw me derive it.

978
00:50:44,000 --> 00:50:46,000
Was I tricking you?

979
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:47,000
Well, I wasn't.

980
00:50:47,000 --> 00:50:49,000
I mean, I tell you, I don't play tricks.

981
00:50:49,000 --> 00:50:51,000
This is absolutely right.

982
00:50:51,000 --> 00:50:54,000
You don't need to know the probability that

983
00:50:54,000 --> 00:50:57,000
it's in the money to price an option.

984
00:50:57,000 --> 00:51:03,000
Because you can price it out of pure no-arbitrage condition.

985
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:06,000
So, that leads me then to

986
00:51:06,000 --> 00:51:09,000
the famous formula for options pricing,

987
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:13,000
the Black-Scholes option pricing formula,

988
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:15,000
which looks completely different from that,

989
00:51:15,000 --> 00:51:18,000
but it's a kindred because

990
00:51:18,000 --> 00:51:21,000
it relies on the same theory.

991
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:23,000
And there it is.

992
00:51:23,000 --> 00:51:25,000
This was derived

993
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:27,000
in the late 70s by

994
00:51:27,000 --> 00:51:29,000
Fisher Black, or maybe early 70s,

995
00:51:29,000 --> 00:51:32,000
by Fisher Black, who was at

996
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:35,000
MIT at the time, I think,

997
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:40,000
but later went to Goldman Sachs.

998
00:51:40,000 --> 00:51:45,000
And Myron Scholes, who is now

999
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:50,000
in San Francisco, doing very well.

1000
00:51:50,000 --> 00:51:55,000
I see him at our Chicago Mercantile Exchange meeting.

1001
00:51:55,000 --> 00:52:00,000
Fisher Black passed away.

1002
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:03,500
So, it doesn't have the probability that

1003
00:52:03,500 --> 00:52:07,000
the option is in the money either.

1004
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:09,000
But it looks a little different.

1005
00:52:09,000 --> 00:52:10,000
It looks totally different from the

1006
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:11,000
formula that I wrote over there.

1007
00:52:11,000 --> 00:52:17,000
The call price is equal to the share price, S,

1008
00:52:17,000 --> 00:52:21,000
times N of D1,

1009
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:23,000
where D1 is this equation.

1010
00:52:23,000 --> 00:52:24,000
And the call price is

1011
00:52:24,000 --> 00:52:25,000
equal to the share price, S,

1012
00:52:25,000 --> 00:52:29,000
minus E to the minus R, the interest rate,

1013
00:52:29,000 --> 00:52:32,000
times time to maturity, T,

1014
00:52:32,000 --> 00:52:35,000
times the exercise price, times N of D2,

1015
00:52:35,000 --> 00:52:37,000
where this is D2.

1016
00:52:37,000 --> 00:52:39,000
And the N function is

1017
00:52:39,000 --> 00:52:41,000
the cumulative normal distribution function.

1018
00:52:41,000 --> 00:52:44,000
I'm not going to derive all that,

1019
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:46,500
because it involves what's

1020
00:52:46,500 --> 00:52:49,000
called the calculus of variations.

1021
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:53,000
I don't think most of you have learned that.

1022
00:52:53,000 --> 00:52:58,000
In ordinary calculus, we have what's called differentials,

1023
00:52:58,000 --> 00:53:01,000
dy, dx, etc.

1024
00:53:01,000 --> 00:53:05,000
Those are fixed numbers in ordinary calculus.

1025
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:09,000
In the mid-20th century, mathematicians,

1026
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:12,000
notably the Japanese mathematician Ito,

1027
00:53:12,000 --> 00:53:16,000
developed a random version of calculus,

1028
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:19,000
where dx and dy are random variables.

1029
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:22,000
That's called the stochastic calculus.

1030
00:53:22,000 --> 00:53:27,000
But I'm not going to derive this.

1031
00:53:27,000 --> 00:53:30,500
But you can see how

1032
00:53:30,500 --> 00:53:34,000
to price an option using black-Scholes.

1033
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:37,000
But black-Scholes is derived, again,

1034
00:53:37,000 --> 00:53:40,000
by the no-arbitrage condition,

1035
00:53:40,000 --> 00:53:42,000
and it doesn't have the probability.

1036
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:45,000
Oh, the other variable that's significant here is sigma,

1037
00:53:45,000 --> 00:53:48,000
which is the standard deviation of

1038
00:53:48,000 --> 00:53:51,000
the change in the stock price.

1039
00:53:51,000 --> 00:53:53,000
So, once we put that in, someone could say,

1040
00:53:53,000 --> 00:53:55,000
well, but probabilities are getting

1041
00:53:55,000 --> 00:53:57,000
in through the back door,

1042
00:53:57,000 --> 00:54:01,000
because this is really a probability-weighted sum

1043
00:54:01,000 --> 00:54:04,000
of the changes in stock prices.

1044
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:07,000
Well, probabilities were not really in here at all,

1045
00:54:07,000 --> 00:54:11,000
but maybe there's something like standard deviation

1046
00:54:11,000 --> 00:54:15,000
even in this equation, because we had cu and cd,

1047
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:18,000
and that gives you some sense of the variability.

1048
00:54:18,000 --> 00:54:20,000
But I'm willing to go on.

1049
00:54:21,000 --> 00:54:23,000
I'm going to leave this equation

1050
00:54:23,000 --> 00:54:25,000
just for you to look at.

1051
00:54:25,000 --> 00:54:30,000
But what it does do is, it shows the stock,

1052
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:33,000
the option price as a nice curve, a linear relationship,

1053
00:54:33,000 --> 00:54:36,000
just like the one I drew by hand,

1054
00:54:36,000 --> 00:54:44,000
which then, as time to exercise goes down,

1055
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:46,000
as we get close to the exercise date,

1056
00:54:46,000 --> 00:54:48,500
that curve eventually coincides

1057
00:54:48,500 --> 00:54:51,000
with the broken straight line.

1058
00:54:51,000 --> 00:55:00,000
So, now I wanted to tell you about implied volatility.

1059
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:04,000
This equation can be used either of two ways.

1060
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:06,000
The most normal way to

1061
00:55:06,000 --> 00:55:08,000
do it, to use this equation,

1062
00:55:08,000 --> 00:55:11,000
is to get the price that you

1063
00:55:11,000 --> 00:55:14,000
think is the right price for an option,

1064
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:16,000
to decide whether I'm paying too

1065
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:18,000
much or too little for an option.

1066
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:20,000
So, with this formula,

1067
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:23,000
I can plug in all the numbers.

1068
00:55:23,000 --> 00:55:25,000
To use this formula,

1069
00:55:25,000 --> 00:55:26,000
I have to know what

1070
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:27,000
the stock price is, that's S,

1071
00:55:27,000 --> 00:55:30,000
I have to know what the exercise price is,

1072
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:32,000
and I have to know what the time to maturity.

1073
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:34,000
These are all specified by

1074
00:55:34,000 --> 00:55:36,000
the stock price and the contract.

