1078
00:55:42,000 --> 00:55:46,000
of the change in the option of the stock price,

1079
00:55:46,000 --> 00:55:49,000
then I can get an option price.

1080
00:55:49,000 --> 00:55:51,000
But I can also turn it around,

1081
00:55:51,000 --> 00:55:53,000
if I already know what the

1082
00:55:53,000 --> 00:55:55,000
option is selling for in the market,

1083
00:55:55,000 --> 00:56:01,000
and I can infer what the implied sigma is, right?

1084
00:56:01,000 --> 00:56:02,500
Because all the other numbers

1085
00:56:02,500 --> 00:56:04,000
in the Black-Scholes formula are clear.

1086
00:56:04,000 --> 00:56:07,000
They're in the newspaper or they're in the option contract.

1087
00:56:07,000 --> 00:56:11,000
There's this one hard to pin down variable.

1088
00:56:11,000 --> 00:56:14,000
What is the variability of the stock price?

1089
00:56:14,000 --> 00:56:18,000
And so, what people often use the Black-Scholes formula to,

1090
00:56:18,000 --> 00:56:20,000
