1095
00:56:33,000 --> 00:56:35,000
Well, it must be that people think,

1096
00:56:35,000 --> 00:56:37,000
and going back to the old interpretation,

1097
00:56:37,000 --> 00:56:40,000
that the probability of exercise is high.

1098
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:43,000
Right? If an out-of-the-money call is valuable,

1099
00:56:43,000 --> 00:56:46,000
it must be people think that sigma is high.

1100
00:56:46,000 --> 00:56:50,000
So, let's actually solve for how high that is.

1101
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:53,000
I can't actually solve this equation.

1102
00:56:53,000 --> 00:56:55,000
I have to do it numerically.

1103
00:56:55,000 --> 00:56:58,000
But I can calculate for any call price,

1104
00:56:58,000 --> 00:57:02,000
given the stock price, exercise price, time to maturity,

1105
00:57:02,000 --> 00:57:05,000
and interest rate, I can calculate what

1106
00:57:05,000 --> 00:57:10,000
volatility would imply that stock price.

1107
00:57:10,000 --> 00:57:16,000
And so, that's where we are with Black-Scholes.

1108
00:57:16,000 --> 00:57:20,000
So, implied volatility,

1109
00:57:20,000 --> 00:57:24,000
is the options market's opinion

1110
00:57:24,000 --> 00:57:28,000
as to how variable the stock market will be

1111
00:57:28,000 --> 00:57:31,000
between now and the exercise date.

1112
00:57:31,000 --> 00:57:37,000
So, one thing we can do is compute implied volatility.

1113
00:57:37,000 --> 00:57:42,000
And I have that here on this chart here.

